---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import { people } from "../lib/people.js";
const au = people.filter((p) => /Australia|Port Pirie|Broken Hill|Whyalla|Quorn|Solomontown|Port Germein/i
  .test(`${p.bornPlace} ${p.diedPlace}`)).sort((a, b) => (a.byear || 9999) - (b.byear || 9999));
---
<Base title="Port Pirie"
  eyebrow="The Australian Žubrinići, from about 1884"
  dek="One man left Senj for a lead-smelting town on the far side of the world, and his name changed on arrival. Twenty of his descendants are in this tree, and every one of them is a Zubrinich.">

  <section class="blk">
    <p class="lede">The first of them is
    <a href={u("/people/joannes-juan-ivan-ive-john-zubrinic/")}><strong>Joannes / Juan / Ivan / Ive /
    John Žubrinić</strong></a> — a man carried in this tree under
    <strong>five versions of one given name</strong> — <strong>born 1860</strong> and
    <strong>died 1930 at Port Pirie, South Australia</strong>. His eldest recorded child is born
    there in <strong>1885</strong>, which puts his arrival at about <strong>1884</strong>.</p>
    <p>From that child onward the surname in the register is <strong>Zubrinich</strong>. Not once
    does it revert. <a href={u("/name/")}>The names →</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>Why Port Pirie</h2>
    <p>Port Pirie is not a random landfall. Smelting began there in <strong>1889</strong>, and it
    grew into one of the largest <strong>lead smelters in the world</strong> — heavy, dangerous,
    continuous work that paid, and that hired men without English. Croatians began arriving in South
    Australia from the Dalmatian islands in the <strong>1890s</strong>, and many of them went to
    <strong>Port Pirie and Broken Hill</strong>. Further groups came with the economic depression in
    Dalmatia and Međimurje between <strong>1910 and 1913</strong>, and again after the political
    unrest of <strong>1922</strong>.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:1rem">The family's later moves confirm the pattern exactly: this tree
    records Zubrinich deaths at <strong>Broken Hill</strong> and <strong>Whyalla</strong> — the
    silver-lead mine and the steelworks. They followed the metal.</p>
    <div class="note"><strong>An anomaly worth flagging.</strong> Ivan's birthplace is entered as
    <em class="mono">“Senj, Split-Dalmatia, Croatia”</em>. Senj is in <strong>Lika-Senj</strong>
    county, not Split-Dalmatia; this is an automated place-match error, and it matters because it is
    the kind of thing that later gets read as evidence of Dalmatian origin.
    <a href={u("/open-questions/")}>Open questions →</a></div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>The Australian records in this tree</h2>
    <p class="dek">{au.length} people with an Australian birth or death place. The infant deaths —
    Robert Emmett in 1888, Susannah in 1889, Stanley in 1897 — are the ordinary arithmetic of a
    smelter town.</p>
    <table class="reg">
      <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Lived</th><th>Born</th><th>Died</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{au.map((p) => (
        <tr><td><a href={u(`/people/${p.slug}/`)}>{p.name}</a></td>
          <td class="mono">{p.byear || "?"}–{p.dyear || "?"}</td>
          <td class="dim">{p.bornPlace}</td><td class="dim">{p.diedPlace}</td></tr>))}</tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>How this branch joins the family</h2>
    <p class="note"><strong>Not yet proved.</strong> Ivan Žubrinić of Port Pirie is
    <em>in the tree</em>, and he is of the right name, the right town and the right generation to be
    close kin of <a href={u("/people/tereza-zubrinic-zubrinic/")}>Tereza Žubrinić</a> (1867–1931),
    Hedviga's grandmother. But the document that joins them — a shared father in the
    <strong>Otočac</strong> or <strong>Senj</strong> baptismal register — has not been produced
    here. Given how rare the name is (<a href={u("/name/")}>about 312 bearers on earth</a>), the
    relationship is very likely. “Very likely” is not “shown”.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Added 13 September 2026</div>
    <h2>And they did not only go to Australia</h2>
    <p class="lede">This page exists because the family knew about Port Pirie. Searching the
    <strong>unanglicised</strong> spelling — <span class="mono">Zubrinic</span> rather than
    <span class="mono">Zubrinich</span> — turns up a <strong>second, entirely separate</strong>
    emigration: to <strong>Youngstown, Johnstown and Brooklyn</strong>, and a
    <a href={u("/kalanj")}>Kalanj</a> emigration to the mining towns of Colorado, Michigan and
    Illinois.</p>
    <p>Nobody in this family knew about any of it, and the records were never scarce — they were
    simply never looked for, because one spelling was searched and the other was not.
    <a href={u("/america")}>The other emigration →</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Added 13 September 2026</div>
    <h2>Nor only to South Australia</h2>
    <p class="lede">Every one of the {au.length} Australians above is in <strong>South Australia</strong>
    or, later, at <strong>Broken Hill</strong>. That is the whole Australian story this archive has
    told. Queensland's own historical index was searched for the first time on 13 September 2026,
    and it holds <strong>one</strong> Zubrinich.</p>

    <p><strong>Stanley Joseph Zubrinich</strong> married <strong>Joanna Marian Josephine
    Clowes</strong> in Queensland on <strong>15 November 1947</strong> — registration
    <span class="mono">1947/B/15067</span>. He is not in this tree, and no Stanley Joseph is.</p>

    <p class="note"><strong>A candidate, not a relative.</strong> Nothing here shows how he joins.
    But two things make him worth the next hour somebody spends. The surname is
    <a href={u("/name/")}>vanishingly rare</a>, and the anglicised <span class="mono">-ich</span>
    ending is the specific spelling this family adopted on arrival at Port Pirie and never once
    reverted from — so a Zubrinich in Australia is far more likely to be one of these than to be
    a separate landing. And the given name is the family's: a <strong>Stanley</strong> was born at
    Port Pirie in 1896 and died there in 1897, and these are people who re-used the names of
    children they lost. A Stanley Joseph marrying in 1947 would have been born around the 1920s,
    which is exactly the generation of <a href={u("/people/terrence-joseph-zubrinich/")}>Terrence
    Joseph</a> (1924) and <a href={u("/people/maurice-william-zubrinich/")}>Maurice William</a>
    (1922). His marriage certificate would name his parents and settle it.</p>

    <p>The nulls are worth recording with it. Queensland's register covers births to 1926, marriages
    to 1951 and deaths to 1996, and across that span it holds no
    <span class="mono">Zubrinic</span>, no <span class="mono">Blazevic</span> and no
    <span class="mono">Subrinich</span>. The three <span class="mono">Blazevich</span> entries are a
    Queensland household of Thomas Bogin Blazevich, who married Jane Chambers in 1940, and are
    unconnected to anyone here.</p>
  </section>

</Base>
